      ],
      "crop": null,
      "created": null
    },
    {
      "id": "EkmHu66MgMG2c45XQLWKw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 82.45812991897742,
      "y": 3128.8165757430506,
      "width": 799.4713134765625,
      "height": 80,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bC9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1711782609,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 859455345,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685318,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This immediately raises a practical question: how could we build a table that accounts for all of these\npossibilities? One option is to **discretize the state space**, but even then the number of possible\nvalues can remain extremely large, making tabular solutions **impractical** because of the size of the\nrequired tables.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This immediately raises a practical question: how could we build a table that accounts for all of these possibilities? One option is to **discretize the state space**, but even then the number of possible values can remain extremely large, making tabular solutions **impractical** because of the size of the required tables.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8C3cYeO67jGrLOdeLYJX2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 85.2981872920243,
      "y": 3248.8165757430506,
      "width": 793.7911987304688,
      "height": 80,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 729994943,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1679846431,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The issue is not only memory. It is also the **time needed to fill those tables accurately**. In many\nrealistic tasks, almost every state the agent encounters will be new. To act sensibly, the agent must\ntherefore **generalize** from previous experience with other states that are, in some relevant sense,\nsimilar to the current one.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The issue is not only memory. It is also the **time needed to fill those tables accurately**. In many realistic tasks, almost every state the agent encounters will be new. To act sensibly, the agent must therefore **generalize** from previous experience with other states that are, in some relevant sense, similar to the current one.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b82rjKj7C7t67Mihu9uPJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 85.48217777053992,
      "y": 3368.8165757430506,
      "width": 793.4232177734375,
      "height": 80,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1674197169,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1032005969,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Tabular methods are inefficient precisely because they lack this ability to generalize. They treat\nstates as separate entries, which prevents them from discovering underlying relationships among\nstates. The key question, then, is how experience gathered from a limited subset of the state space\ncan be used to produce a useful approximation over a much larger one.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Tabular methods are inefficient precisely because they lack this ability to generalize. They treat states as separate entries, which prevents them from discovering underlying relationships among states. The key question, then, is how experience gathered from a limited subset of the state space can be used to produce a useful approximation over a much larger one.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "iAi-4zOQooUakFHto0QtC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 83.90625003616492,
      "y": 3508.8165757430506,
      "width": 796.5750732421875,
      "height": 40,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1066903263,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 781946943,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Under these conditions, we should not expect to find truly optimal policies. A more realistic goal is to\nfind **good approximate solutions** using **limited computational resources**.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Under these conditions, we should not expect to find truly optimal policies. A more realistic goal is to find **good approximate solutions** using **limited computational resources**.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "EnSQ0wdfKci3mBCMaj7BU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 90.37808231155554,
      "y": 3578.8165757430506,
      "width": 783.6314086914062,
      "height": 60,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1682045585,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1188288305,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**Generalization from examples** has already been studied extensively, so we do not need to invent\nentirely new methods for reinforcement learning. Instead, we can combine reinforcement learning\nalgorithms with existing generalization techniques.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**Generalization from examples** has already been studied extensively, so we do not need to invent entirely new methods for reinforcement learning. Instead, we can combine reinforcement learning algorithms with existing generalization techniques.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "MOT8EZOtPzvbZX2qvTmgy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 86.29010013382117,
      "y": 3678.8165757430506,
      "width": 791.807373046875,
      "height": 60,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1960669951,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 576532575,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686671,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This kind of generalization is usually called **function approximation**. The idea is to take examples\nfrom a target function, such as a value function, and use them to construct an approximation of the\nfull function. In this sense, function approximation is a form of **supervised learning**.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This kind of generalization is usually called **function approximation**. The idea is to take examples from a target function, such as a value function, and use them to construct an approximation of the full function. In this sense, function approximation is a form of **supervised learning**.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "OexJ_9Y8fuYnRY_lCWeSa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 92.43414310257117,
      "y": 3778.8165757430506,
      "width": 779.519287109375,
      "height": 60,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1747353713,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 563333393,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791212686672,
      "created": 1791212685319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In principle, almost any supervised learning method can be used as a function approximator inside a\nreinforcement learning algorithm, although in practice some methods fit this role more naturally than\nothers.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
